faq:
  - q: "What is Stripe's hiring process?"
    a: "Stripe's process: (1) Application or recruiter screen. (2) Recruiter phone screen — background, culture fit, role overview. (3) Technical or role-specific screen. (4) Onsite or virtual loop — 4–6 rounds covering technical skills, systems thinking, communication, and values. (5) Reference checks (Stripe takes references seriously — expect 3–5 references to be called). (6) Offer decision. Stripe is known for a rigorous reference check process that is more substantive than most companies. The full process typically takes 4–8 weeks."
  - q: "What does Stripe look for in candidates?"
    a: "Stripe explicitly states its hiring bar: they hire people who are 'craftspeople who care deeply about the quality of what they build.' Key attributes: (1) Intelligence and curiosity — Stripe values raw intellectual horsepower and genuine curiosity about hard problems. (2) Strong written communication — Stripe's culture is writing-heavy (memos, documentation, async communication). (3) User empathy — understanding the developer and business customer deeply. (4) Integrity and directness — Stripe's culture values honest, direct communication over diplomatic hedging."
  - q: "How are Stripe's software engineering interviews structured?"
    a: "Stripe's SWE interviews include: (1) Coding rounds — typically two, using LeetCode medium-hard difficulty questions. Stripe's coding environment uses a specific setup — practice on their exact platform if given access. (2) System design — at senior levels, design distributed payment systems with reliability and correctness constraints. (3) Bug squash — Stripe sometimes includes a round where you debug a real-world scenario in a codebase similar to Stripe's. (4) Cultural/values rounds — assessing curiosity, communication, and user empathy. Stripe's technical bar is very high — the company pays top of market and expects top of market performance."
  - q: "Does Stripe have a written component in interviews?"
    a: "Yes. Stripe's culture values excellent writing, and many roles include a written component — either as part of the application (a cover letter that is actually read) or as a take-home or in-person exercise. PM and non-technical roles often include a written case study. Engineering roles may include written documentation of a technical approach. Practice clear, structured written communication before Stripe interviews."
  - q: "How competitive is it to get a job at Stripe?"
    a: "Very competitive. Stripe is consistently rated among the best places to work in tech, pays top-of-market compensation, and has a strong brand in the developer community. The acceptance rate is estimated under 1% of applicants. Stripe has also gone through hiring freezes and layoffs in 2023, which tightened the market further. However, Stripe actively recruits through its developer community events, academic partnerships, and referral network — these pathways have higher conversion than cold applications."
